      'canonical' => '/general-triangle-solver',
      'content' => '<h2>The Universal Triangle Solver: Solving Every SSS, SAS, and ASA Scenario</h2>
            <p>Triangles are the building blocks of geometry and the foundation of modern engineering. Unlike right-angled triangles that can be solved with simple SOH-CAH-TOA trigonometry, a "general" triangle requires more sophisticated tools like the Law of Sines and the Law of Cosines. Our General Triangle Solver is designed to take any three known properties—sides or angles—and instantly calculate everything else, including area, perimeter, and the classification of the triangle.</p>
            
            <h3>The SSS Scenario: Side-Side-Side</h3>
            <p>When you know all three side lengths (a, b, and c), the most critical step is determining the individual angles. We use the Law of Cosines (e.g., cosA = (b² + c² - a²) / 2bc) to find the first angle, then the Law of Sines to find the second, and finally the 180-degree rule for the third. A crucial check in this mode is the "Triangle Inequality Theorem," which states that the sum of any two sides must be strictly greater than the third side. If this isn\'t met, the triangle cannot exist in 2D space.</p>
            
            <h3>SAS and ASA: Building the Rest</h3>
            <p>In the SAS (Side-Angle-Side) mode, you define a corner and the two arms stretching from it. We use the Law of Cosines to find the "closing" side and then solve the rest as an SSS problem. In the ASA (Angle-Side-Angle) mode, we find the missing third angle immediately (180 - A - B) and then use the Law of Sines to find the remaining side lengths. These methods are the bread and butter of traditional surveying and navigation.</p>
            
            <h3>Applying Heron’s Formula for Area</h3>
            <p>Once all sides are known, calculating the area is most efficiently done using Heron\'s Formula. This elegant method uses the semi-perimeter (s = half of the perimeter) and the formula: Area = √(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)). This avoids the need to calculate a vertical height, allowing for a pure side-based solution that is both fast and incredibly accurate.</p>
            
            <h3>Applications in the Modern World</h3>
            <p>Triangulation is the principle that allows your mobile phone to determine your location by measuring angles to known cell towers. In architecture, triangles are the only polygons that are "intrinsically rigid," meaning they won\'t deform unless their side lengths change. This is why you see triangular trusses in everything from backyard sheds to massive suspension bridges like the Golden Gate. In computer graphics, every complex characters or landscape you see on screen is actually a "mesh" of millions of tiny triangles rendered in real-time.</p>
            
            <h3>Solving a Practice Problem</h3>
            <p>Suppose you have a triangle with sides 7, 10, and 5.
            1. <strong>Check Inequality:</strong> 5 + 7 = 12 (Greater than 10). Possible!
            2. <strong>Calculate Perimeter:</strong> 7 + 10 + 5 = 22.
            3. <strong>Find Semi-perimeter (s):</strong> 11.
            4. <strong>Calculate Area:</strong> √(11 × (11-7) × (11-10) × (11-5)) = √(11 × 4 × 1 × 6) = √264 ≈ 16.248 sq units.
            In just a few steps, you transformed three numbers into a complete geographic description of the shape.</p>',
