      'canonical' => '/golden-rectangle-calculator',
      'content' => '<h2>The Golden Rectangle: A Legacy of Proportional Beauty</h2>
            <p>The Golden Rectangle is a unique shape whose sides are in the "Golden Ratio"—approximately 1.618 to 1. For millennia, this specific proportion has been celebrated as the most aesthetically pleasing shape possible for a rectangle. From the facade of the Parthenon in ancient Greece to the dimensions of a modern credit card, the Golden Rectangle appears whenever balance, harmony, and structural elegance are the primary goals of design.</p>
            
            <h3>The Mathematics of Phi (φ)</h3>
            <p>The secret behind the Golden Rectangle is the irrational number Phi (φ), which equals (1 + √5) / 2. This ratio has a fascinating property: if you cut a square out of a Golden Rectangle, the remaining piece is another, identical Golden Rectangle (just smaller). This self-similarity can be repeated forever, creating a fractal pattern that is the basis for the famous Golden Spiral seen in nautilus shells and spiral galaxies.</p>
            
            <h3>Why is it so "pleasing"?</h3>
            <p>Psychologists and art historians have long debated why humans are drawn to this ratio. Some suggest it mimics the proportions of the human body, while others believe our brains are wired to process information more efficiently when it follows this specific flow. Whether it is biological or cultural, the "Golden" label has stood the test of time, appearing in the works of Leonardo da Vinci, the layout of the Great Pyramids, and the branding of the world\'s most valuable tech companies.</p>
            
            <h3>Calculating Gold: From One Side to Many</h3>
            <p>Our calculator removes the guesswork. If you have a specific width in mind and want it to be "Golden," you simply input that number. If it\'s the short side, we multiply by 1.618; if it\'s the long side, we divide. This ensures that your canvas, your website header, or your custom furniture piece follows the same mathematical laws that govern the growth of a sunflower or the shape of a storm cloud.</p>
            
            <h3>Applications in Modern Design</h3>
            <p>In Web Design, the Golden Ratio is used to determine the ratio between the main content area and the sidebar to ensure a balanced, professional "SaaS-look." In Graphic Design, it helps place key elements (like logos) in spots that the eye naturally gravitates toward. In Product Design, it is often the secret behind why a particular smartphone or car "just looks right" compared to its competitors. It provides a blueprint for consistency that transcends trends and decades.</p>
            
            <h3>Example Calculation</h3>
            <p>If you are building a window and the height is fixed at 120cm:
            1. <strong>To find the "Golden" Width (Short Side):</strong> 120 / 1.618 = 74.16cm.
            2. <strong>To find the "Golden" Width (Long Side):</strong> 120 × 1.618 = 194.16cm.
            By choosing one of these two widths, you guarantee that your window follows the proportions used by the world\'s greatest architects for centuries.</p>',
