      'canonical' => '/golden-section-calculator',
      'content' => '<h2>The Golden Section: Dividing Lines with Mathematical Perfection</h2>
            <p>The Golden Section is a specific way of dividing a line segment such that the ratio of the whole line to the longer part is the same as the ratio of the longer part to the shorter part. This mathematical concept, also known as the "Extreme and Mean Ratio," is the 1D version of the Golden Ratio. It is the fundamental law of proportion that defines how parts relate to the whole, ensuring that no segment looks too dominant or too insignificant.</p>
            
            <h3>Understanding the Proportion</h3>
            <p>If a line of length L is divided into a longer part (a) and a shorter part (b), the Golden Section occurs when L/a = a/b = 1.618... This creates a perfect equilibrium where the growth from small to large follows a consistent, geometric progression. This is the same logic found in the Fibonacci sequence, where every number is the sum of the two before it; as the numbers get larger, their ratio approaches this exact Golden proportion.</p>
            
            <h3>Historical and Artistic roots</h3>
            <p>The term "Divine Proportion" was first used during the Renaissance to describe the Golden Section. It was believed to reflect the mind of God in nature. Artists used the Golden Section to decide where to place horizons in landscape paintings or where to position the subject in a portrait. By placing the most important element of a painting at the Golden Section mark—rather than in the dead center—painters created a sense of dynamic energy and natural balance that is still taught in art schools today.</p>
            
            <h3>Applications in Science and Engineering</h3>
            <p>Beyond art, the Golden Section appears in the branching of trees, the arrangement of leaves on a stem, and the spiral of DNA. In engineering, it is used in the study of "quasi-crystals" and the analysis of structural stability. In finance, some traders use "Fibonacci Retracement" levels—based on the Golden Section—to predict where the price of a stock might bounce or reverse, assuming that human psychological behavior follows the same natural patterns as physical growth.</p>
            
            <h3>Practical Uses for our Calculator</h3>
            <p>Our Golden Section Calculator is an essential tool for creators. If you have a total length of 100 on a page and want to know where the most important visual "accent" should go, the Golden Section will tell you exactly where the "energy center" of the line is. Architects use it to decide the placement of doors and windows along a wall, and interior designers use it to decide the height of wainscoting or the placement of shelving units.</p>
            
            <h3>Quick Start Example</h3>
            <p>If you have a wall that is 300cm wide and want to place a decorative pillar at the Golden Section:
            1. <strong>Long Segment (a):</strong> 300 / 1.618 ≈ 185.4cm.
            2. <strong>Short Segment (b):</strong> 300 - 185.4 ≈ 114.6cm.
            By placing the pillar 185.4cm from one side, you create a division that feels naturally balanced to the human eye, avoiding the static feel of a center-split.</p>',
