    'concrete-slab-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'concrete-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Concrete Slab Calculator',
      'title' => 'Concrete Slab Calculator - Free Online Estimation Tool | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free Concrete Slab Calculator to estimate concrete volume, bags needed, and project costs. Accurate results for slabs, footings, walls, and more.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Our Concrete Slab Calculator is designed to help contractors, DIY enthusiasts, and homeowners accurately estimate the amount of concrete required for slab pouring projects. Whether you are building a patio, driveway, garage floor, or any flat concrete surface, this tool simplifies the complex math of volume calculation into an instant, reliable result.

Calculating concrete volume involves multiplying length × width × thickness and converting the result into cubic yards or cubic feet. Most ready-mix concrete suppliers require orders in cubic yards, while bagged concrete (such as Quikrete or Sakrete) lists coverage in cubic feet per bag. Our Concrete Slab Calculator handles both measurement systems seamlessly — simply enter your dimensions in imperial or metric units and get precise results instantly.

A common mistake in concrete estimation is failing to account for waste and overfill. Industry best practice recommends adding 5–10%% extra to your calculated volume to cover spillage, form irregularities, and subgrade absorption. Our calculator automatically includes a 5%% waste buffer, ensuring you order enough material the first time. Under-ordering concrete can be catastrophic — a mid-pour shortage means cold joints, structural weakness, and wasted labor.

The density of standard concrete is approximately 150 pounds per cubic foot (2,400 kg/m³). This means a typical 4-inch thick, 10×10 foot slab requires about 1.23 cubic yards of concrete — or roughly 56 bags of 80lb Quikrete mix. Understanding these numbers helps you plan delivery logistics, labor scheduling, and budget allocation. Whether you need a slab pouring projects estimate for a small backyard project or a commercial foundation, accurate calculation is the foundation of every successful pour.

Our tool supports imperial and metric units, multiple bag sizes (40lb, 60lb, 80lb, 25kg, 40kg), and provides instant results with no signup required. Use the related tools below to plan your full construction project — from square footage to gravel base calculations.',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How much concrete do I need for a 10x10 slab?',
          'a' => 'At 4 inches thick, a 10×10 ft slab requires about 1.23 cubic yards or 56 bags of 80lb concrete mix (including 5% waste).',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What thickness should a concrete slab be?',
          'a' => 'Residential patios and sidewalks: 4 inches. Driveways: 4–6 inches. Garage floors: 4–6 inches. Commercial floors: 6+ inches.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How many bags of concrete for a 4x4 slab?',
          'a' => 'A 4×4 foot slab at 4 inches deep needs about 0.20 cubic yards — approximately 9 bags of 80lb or 12 bags of 60lb mix.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should I use ready-mix or bagged concrete?',
          'a' => 'For projects under 1 cubic yard, bagged concrete is practical. Over 1 yard, ready-mix delivery is more efficient and cost-effective.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need rebar in my concrete slab?',
          'a' => 'Yes, rebar or wire mesh reinforcement is recommended for almost all slabs to prevent cracking and improve load capacity.',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the strongest concrete mix for slabs?',
          'a' => 'Standard 3,000 PSI is sufficient for most residential slabs. For heavy-duty use (garages, driveways), use 4,000 PSI or higher.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
