      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Our Concrete Block Calculator helps you estimate the concrete and mortar needed for block wall and masonry projects. Concrete blocks (CMU — Concrete Masonry Units) are fundamental building materials for walls, foundations, fences, and retaining structures. This calculator determines how many blocks, bags of mortar, and cubic yards of fill concrete your project requires.

Standard concrete blocks measure 8×8×16 inches (nominal). A wall requires approximately 1.125 blocks per square foot of wall area. For a typical 8-foot high, 20-foot long wall, you need about 180 blocks, plus mortar joints at 3/8 inch. Our calculator computes block count and the concrete fill volume for grouted cells.

Grouted (filled) block walls require concrete fill in the hollow cores. Each standard 8-inch block has approximately 0.50 cubic feet of void space. For a fully grouted wall, this adds significant concrete volume — a 160-square-foot wall requires approximately 90 cubic feet (3.33 cubic yards) of grout fill. Our Concrete Block Calculator accounts for both the block count and the fill volume.

Mortar requirements average about 7 bags of Type S mortar per 100 blocks. We recommend Type S mortar for structural and below-grade applications, and Type N for above-grade non-load-bearing walls. Accurate estimation of both blocks and mortar prevents costly jobsite delays and return trips to the supply yard.

Whether you are building a garden wall, a retaining structure, or a full masonry building, our free online calculator gives you professional-grade estimates in seconds.',
