      ),
      'article' => 'Knowing the weight of concrete is essential for weight estimation per cubic yard. Our Concrete Weight Calculator converts volume measurements into weight estimates using the standard density of 150 pounds per cubic foot (2,400 kg/m³). This information is critical for structural engineering, transportation planning, and load-bearing calculations.

Standard concrete weighs approximately 150 lb/ft³ when cured. Fresh concrete is slightly heavier due to water content — about 3,600–4,000 pounds per cubic yard. Lightweight concrete (made with expanded shale or clay aggregate) weighs 90–120 lb/ft³, while heavyweight concrete (used in radiation shielding) can exceed 300 lb/ft³.

Understanding concrete weight is crucial for several real-world applications: calculating floor load capacity, sizing crane and pump equipment, determining truck load limits, and designing formwork to withstand wet concrete pressure. A standard ready-mix truck carries 8–10 cubic yards, meaning a full load weighs 30,000–40,000 pounds.

Our Concrete Weight Calculator takes your project dimensions (length, width, thickness) and calculates total volume, then converts to estimated weight using standard density. The tool includes a 5%% waste buffer and supports both imperial and metric units. For elevated slabs and suspended structures, always consult a structural engineer for load calculations.

Accurate weight estimation also helps with material handling logistics — knowing that a 4-inch thick, 10×10 foot slab weighs about 5,000 pounds helps you plan equipment needs and ensure your subgrade can support the load.',
