      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Calculate precisely how much concrete you need for house and building foundation volume with our Concrete Foundation Calculator. Footings are the structural backbone of any building — from residential decks to commercial foundations. Getting the volume right is not optional, it is critical to structural safety and code compliance.

Standard residential footings are typically 12–24 inches wide and 8–12 inches deep, extending below the frost line in cold climates. A continuous footing for a 40-foot perimeter wall at 18 inches wide and 12 inches deep requires approximately 2.22 cubic yards of concrete. Our calculator handles these dimensions effortlessly — enter length, width, and depth to get instant results in both cubic feet and cubic yards.

Footing calculations differ from slab calculations because footings are often linear rather than area-based. For isolated pad footings (used under columns and posts), the calculation is length × width × depth. For continuous strip footings, you measure the total linear length × width × depth. Our tool supports both approaches by letting you enter any combination of dimensions.

Building codes typically require footings to extend at least 12 inches below undisturbed soil and 12 inches below the frost line. The minimum compressive strength for residential footings is 2,500 PSI, though 3,000–3,500 PSI is commonly specified. These specifications affect both the volume and type of concrete you order.

Accurate footing calculations prevent two common problems: over-ordering (wasting money on excess concrete) and under-ordering (compromising structural integrity with cold joints). Our free Concrete Foundation Calculator includes a 5%% waste buffer and supports imperial and metric measurements.',
