    'how-to-calculate-gpa' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'gpa-calculator',
      'h1' => 'How to Calculate GPA',
      'title' => 'How to Calculate GPA - Free Online Tool | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free How to Calculate GPA to calculate your grade point average instantly. Supports weighted GPA, cumulative GPA, and semester GPA on the standard 4.0 scale.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Understanding the GPA formula empowers you to take control of your academic future. Our How to Calculate GPA guide walks you through learning GPA calculation step by step clearly and completely. The Grade Point Average is a standardized numerical representation of your academic performance, calculated using a specific mathematical formula that weights grades by credit hours.

The basic GPA formula has four steps: (1) Convert each letter grade to its point value on the 4.0 scale: A=4.0, B=3.0, C=2.0, D=1.0, F=0.0. (2) Multiply each grade point value by the course credit hours to get quality points. (3) Add all quality points together. (4) Divide total quality points by total credit hours. Example: Math (A, 4 credits) = 16 quality points; English (B+, 3 credits) = 9.9 points; Biology (A-, 4 credits) = 14.8 points. Total: 40.7 / 11 = 3.70 GPA.

For cumulative GPA, repeat this process across all semesters. Include every course from every term in your calculation. Some schools exclude transfer credits, repeated courses, or pass/fail courses from GPA calculations. Check your institution\'s specific policies if your manual calculation differs from your transcript.

Our free online GPA Calculator automates this entire process. Enter course names, select grades from the dropdown, input credit hours, and the tool computes your GPA instantly. Add multiple semesters for cumulative tracking. No math required, no signup needed.',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the GPA formula?',
          'a' => 'GPA = Total Quality Points / Total Credit Hours. Quality Points = Grade Value x Course Credits. Add quality points for all courses and divide by total credits.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I convert letter grades to GPA?',
          'a' => 'Use the standard scale: A=4.0, A-=3.7, B+=3.3, B=3.0, B-=2.7, C+=2.3, C=2.0, C-=1.7, D+=1.3, D=1.0, F=0.0.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What are quality points?',
          'a' => 'Quality points are the product of a course grade value and its credit hours. A 3-credit course with a B (3.0) grade earns 9.0 quality points.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do pass/fail courses affect GPA?',
          'a' => 'Generally no. Pass/fail (P/F) courses typically earn credit but do not factor into GPA calculations. Check your school specific policy.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I calculate GPA for a single class?',
          'a' => 'The GPA for a single class is simply the grade point value: A=4.0, B=3.0, etc. GPA becomes meaningful when averaging across multiple courses.',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between GPA and CGPA?',
          'a' => 'GPA usually refers to a single term. CGPA (Cumulative Grade Point Average) represents all terms combined. In practice, people often use GPA to mean cumulative GPA.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Identify all courses you want to include in your GPA calculation.',
        1 => 'Convert each letter grade to its point value: A=4.0, B=3.0, C=2.0, D=1.0, F=0.0.',
