      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Wondering how new grades will affect your GPA? Our Current GPA Calculator is designed for calculating GPA with existing grades. This tool lets you start from your current cumulative GPA and add new semester courses to project how your GPA will change. It is the perfect planning tool for students who want to set grade targets and understand what it takes to reach their academic goals.

To calculate your projected GPA with new grades: (1) Multiply your current cumulative GPA by your total earned credit hours. This gives your existing total quality points. (2) Add quality points from new courses. (3) Divide the new total quality points by the new total credit hours. Example: Current GPA 3.2 with 60 credits = 192 quality points. Add a semester of 15 credits with a 3.6 GPA = 54 new quality points. New cumulative: (192 + 54) / 75 = 3.28 GPA.

This forward-looking calculation helps you set realistic goals. If your current GPA is 3.0 after 90 credits, you would need straight As (4.0) for an entire 15-credit semester just to raise it to 3.10. Understanding this mathematical reality helps you plan strategically and set achievable semester targets rather than unrealistic cumulative GPA goals.

