    'how-to-calculate-gpa-high-school' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'gpa-calculator',
      'h1' => 'How to Calculate GPA High School',
      'title' => 'How to Calculate GPA High School - Free Online Tool | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free How to Calculate GPA High School to calculate your grade point average instantly. Supports weighted GPA, cumulative GPA, and semester GPA on the standard 4.0 scale.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'High school GPA is a cornerstone of college admissions, and our How to Calculate GPA High School helps students track high school GPA computation guide with precision. Colleges evaluate your transcript holistically, but GPA remains one of the strongest quantitative indicators of academic achievement. Whether you apply to Ivy League schools or state universities, an accurate GPA gives you a clear picture of where you stand.

High school GPA typically uses the standard 4.0 unweighted scale: A = 4.0, B = 3.0, C = 2.0, D = 1.0, F = 0.0. However, many schools also calculate a weighted GPA that gives bonus points for Honors (+0.5) and AP/IB courses (+1.0), creating a 5.0 scale. For example, an A in AP Biology earns 5.0 quality points on the weighted scale versus 4.0 on the unweighted scale. Our calculator supports both approaches.

The average high school GPA nationally is approximately 3.0 on an unweighted scale. Competitive universities (top 50) typically admit students with 3.5+ unweighted or 4.0+ weighted GPAs. Your GPA across all four years matters, though many admissions offices place extra weight on junior and senior year performance. Some schools also consider your core GPA (English, math, science, social studies, foreign language) separately.

Use our tool to enter each course with its grade and credits. Add semesters for each school year to track your progress from freshman through senior year. This helps identify trends, plan course selections, and set realistic goals for college applications.',
