        array (
          'q' => 'How is GPA calculated?',
          'a' => 'GPA is calculated by multiplying each course grade point value by its credit hours, summing all quality points, and dividing by total credit hours. For example: (A x 3 credits + B x 4 credits) / 7 credits = GPA.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a good GPA?',
          'a' => 'A good GPA depends on context. For college: 3.0+ is good, 3.5+ is very good, 3.7+ is excellent. For graduate school admissions, 3.3+ is typically expected.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the highest GPA possible?',
          'a' => 'On the standard unweighted scale, 4.0 is the highest GPA (all As). On weighted scales that include AP/Honors courses, GPAs can exceed 4.0, up to 5.0.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does GPA matter for getting a job?',
          'a' => 'Some employers screen for minimum GPA (often 3.0-3.5), especially for entry-level positions. After 2-3 years of work experience, GPA becomes less relevant.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I raise my GPA after a bad semester?',
          'a' => 'Yes, but the more credits you have accumulated, the harder it is to change your cumulative GPA. Earning straight As in a 15-credit semester adds about 0.1 to your cumulative GPA.',
