          'q' => 'How do I project my GPA with new grades?',
          'a' => 'Multiply your current GPA by total credits for existing quality points. Add new course quality points. Divide new total by new total credits.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What grades do I need to raise my GPA?',
          'a' => 'Use our calculator: enter your current GPA and credits, then add courses with target grades to see the projected cumulative GPA.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How much can one semester change my GPA?',
          'a' => 'This depends on total credits earned. After 30 credits, a 15-credit semester can shift GPA by up to 0.3 points. After 90 credits, the same semester shifts only about 0.15 points.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I recover from a 2.0 GPA?',
          'a' => 'Yes, but it takes time. If you have 30 credits at 2.0, earning a 3.5 for the next 30 credits would bring your cumulative to 2.75. Consistent improvement is key.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should I retake a course to improve my GPA?',
          'a' => 'Check your schools grade replacement policy. Some schools substitute the new grade, others include both. Retaking a D (1.0) and earning an A (4.0) can significantly help.',
        ),
      ),
