    'gpa-calculator-5-point-scale' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'gpa-calculator',
      'h1' => 'GPA Calculator 5 Point Scale',
      'title' => 'GPA Calculator 5 Point Scale - Free Online Tool | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free GPA Calculator 5 Point Scale to calculate your grade point average instantly. Supports weighted GPA, cumulative GPA, and semester GPA on the standard 4.0 scale.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'A weighted GPA rewards academic rigor, and our GPA Calculator 5 Point Scale accurately computes 5-point weighted GPA scale. Many high schools and some colleges use weighted GPA scales to recognize students who challenge themselves with Advanced Placement (AP), International Baccalaureate (IB), and Honors-level coursework. On a typical weighted scale, regular courses max at 4.0, Honors at 4.5, and AP/IB at 5.0.

The weighted GPA formula assigns bonus grade points to advanced courses: an A in a regular course earns 4.0 quality points per credit, while an A in an AP course earns 5.0 quality points per credit. A student with four AP courses all earning As and two regular courses with Bs would calculate: (4x5.0x1 + 2x3.0x1) / 6 = 26/6 = 4.33 weighted GPA. This system incentivizes challenging course selection.

Many top universities consider weighted GPA alongside the rigor of your course load. A 3.8 weighted GPA with six AP courses may be viewed more favorably than a 4.0 unweighted GPA with no advanced coursework. College Board data shows that students who take AP courses and exams are significantly more likely to earn college degrees within four years.

Our calculator handles weighted calculations by allowing you to designate advanced course levels. Enter your courses with their appropriate difficulty level and credits to get both weighted and unweighted GPA estimates. Use this alongside our standard GPA Calculator to understand how course selection impacts your academic profile.',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a weighted GPA?',
          'a' => 'A weighted GPA uses an expanded scale (typically 5.0) that gives extra grade points for advanced courses like AP, IB, and Honors classes, rewarding academic rigor.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How are AP classes weighted in GPA?',
          'a' => 'AP courses typically add 1.0 to the grade point value. An A in AP = 5.0, B in AP = 4.0, etc. Honors courses usually add 0.5 points.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do colleges prefer weighted or unweighted GPA?',
          'a' => 'Most colleges consider both but recalculate on their own scale. They value the rigor shown by weighted GPA but use unweighted for standardized comparison.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can my weighted GPA be above 4.0?',
          'a' => 'Yes, on a 5.0 weighted scale. A student with mostly AP courses earning As could have a weighted GPA of 4.5 or higher.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a 4.5 weighted GPA good?',
          'a' => 'Yes, 4.5 weighted is excellent. It indicates mostly As in a mix of AP/Honors and regular courses, placing you in the top tier of applicants at most universities.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Enter all courses including the course level (AP, Honors, Regular).',
        1 => 'For AP courses, note the grade points are enhanced on the weighted scale.',
        2 => 'Enter the letter grade received in each course.',
        3 => 'Input credit hours for each course (standard is 1 credit per semester).',
        4 => 'The calculator shows your weighted GPA accounting for course difficulty.',
        5 => 'Compare weighted and unweighted GPAs to understand your academic profile.',
      ),
