      'h1' => 'Medical School GPA Calculator',
      'title' => 'Medical School GPA Calculator - Free Online Tool | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free Medical School GPA Calculator to calculate your grade point average instantly. Supports weighted GPA, cumulative GPA, and semester GPA on the standard 4.0 scale.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Science GPA is a critical metric for professional school admissions, and our Medical School GPA Calculator helps you calculate medical school admission GPA accurately. Medical schools, dental schools, nursing programs, and other health professions require a separate science GPA (BCPM: Biology, Chemistry, Physics, Math) alongside your cumulative GPA. This science-specific GPA often carries more weight than your overall average.

AAMC (Association of American Medical Colleges) categorizes courses for science GPA calculation: Biology, Chemistry (including biochemistry), Physics, and Mathematics (including statistics). Only courses that fall into these four categories count toward your science GPA. A student with a 3.8 cumulative GPA but a 3.2 science GPA may find medical school admissions challenging because the science GPA reveals performance in the coursework most relevant to medical education.

Competitive medical school applicants typically have science GPAs of 3.5 or higher. For dental school, the average accepted student has a science GPA around 3.4. Nursing programs vary widely but often require 3.0+ in prerequisite science courses. Understanding your science GPA separately from your overall GPA helps you make informed decisions about whether to retake courses, pursue post-baccalaureate programs, or consider alternative career paths.

Enter only your science courses (biology, chemistry, physics, math) into our calculator to compute your science GPA. Compare this with your cumulative GPA to understand your competitive profile for professional school applications.',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a science GPA?',
          'a' => 'Science GPA (BCPM) includes only Biology, Chemistry, Physics, and Math courses. It is calculated separately from cumulative GPA for medical and dental school applications.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What science GPA do I need for medical school?',
          'a' => 'Competitive applicants typically have 3.5+ science GPA. The average matriculant has approximately 3.65 science GPA. Below 3.0 makes admission very difficult.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does biochemistry count toward science GPA?',
          'a' => 'Yes, biochemistry is categorized under Chemistry for BCPM GPA calculation by AMCAS.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I raise a low science GPA?',
          'a' => 'Yes, through post-baccalaureate programs, special master programs, or retaking courses. A strong upward trend is viewed favorably by admissions committees.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is statistics included in science GPA?',
          'a' => 'Yes, statistics courses are categorized under Mathematics for BCPM science GPA calculations.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Enter ONLY science courses: Biology, Chemistry, Physics, and Mathematics.',
        1 => 'Include labs, lectures, and all sections of qualifying courses.',
        2 => 'Select grades and enter credit hours for each science course.',
        3 => 'Do not include non-BCPM courses like English, History, or Psychology.',
        4 => 'Review your science-specific GPA separate from your cumulative GPA.',
        5 => 'Use this to assess your competitiveness for health profession programs.',
      ),
