    'gpa-calculator-for-graduate-students' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'gpa-calculator',
      'h1' => 'GPA Calculator for Graduate Students',
      'title' => 'GPA Calculator for Graduate Students - Free Online Tool | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free GPA Calculator for Graduate Students to calculate your grade point average instantly. Supports weighted GPA, cumulative GPA, and semester GPA on the standard 4.0 scale.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'College academics revolve around your GPA, and our GPA Calculator for Graduate Students is designed specifically for graduate-level GPA computation. From freshman orientation to graduation day, your grade point average determines scholarship eligibility, academic standing, honors recognition, and post-graduation opportunities. This calculator uses the standard 4.0 scale employed by the vast majority of American colleges and universities.

The college GPA formula multiplies each course grade point value by credit hours to produce quality points: Quality Points = Grade Value x Credit Hours. Your GPA equals Total Quality Points divided by Total Credit Hours. A student taking 15 credits who earns three As (4.0) and two Bs (3.0) would calculate: (3x4.0x3 + 2x3.0x3) / 15 = (36 + 18) / 15 = 3.60 GPA. This credit-weighted system ensures that a 4-credit lecture carries more influence than a 1-credit lab.

Most colleges require a minimum 2.0 GPA for graduation and good standing. Dean\'s List typically requires 3.5 or higher. Graduate school admissions generally expect 3.0+ for master\'s programs and 3.3+ for doctoral programs. Professional schools (medical, law, dental) often have even higher thresholds. Understanding where you stand each semester helps you make informed decisions about course loads, study strategies, and academic goals.

Our calculator handles multi-semester tracking, letting you see both individual semester GPAs and your running cumulative average. Enter courses, grades, and credit hours for each term to build a complete picture of your college academic performance. No signup required.',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What GPA do you need to stay in college?',
          'a' => 'Most colleges require a minimum 2.0 cumulative GPA. Falling below this threshold triggers academic probation, and continued low performance may lead to academic suspension.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How does college GPA affect career prospects?',
          'a' => 'Many employers, especially for entry-level positions, use a 3.0 GPA cutoff. Investment banking, consulting, and tech companies may require 3.5+. After 2-3 years of experience, GPA matters less.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the average college GPA?',
          'a' => 'The national average college GPA is approximately 3.15, though this varies significantly by institution and major. STEM majors tend to have lower averages (2.9-3.1) than humanities (3.2-3.5).',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do colleges use weighted or unweighted GPA?',
          'a' => 'Colleges typically recalculate your GPA on their own scale. Most use unweighted 4.0 for college course grades but may consider weighted high school GPA during admissions.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How many credits do I need to raise my GPA?',
          'a' => 'The impact of new credits depends on your existing total. After 60 credits, a perfect 15-credit semester (4.0) would raise a 3.0 GPA to approximately 3.20.',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does my college GPA include transfer credits?',
          'a' => 'Policies vary. Most colleges accept transfer credits but do not include transfer grades in GPA calculation. Your college GPA typically reflects only courses taken at that institution.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'List all courses from your college semester with their official names.',
        1 => 'Select the letter grade (A through F, with plus/minus) for each course.',
        2 => 'Enter the credit hours assigned to each course (typically 3-4 credits).',
        3 => 'Add additional courses using the Add Course button until all classes are entered.',
        4 => 'For cumulative GPA, add previous semesters using the Add Semester button.',
        5 => 'Review your calculated semester GPA and cumulative GPA in the results panel.',
