    'accuweather-snow-day-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'snow-day-calculator',
      'h1' => 'AccuWeather Snow Day Calculator',
      'title' => 'AccuWeather Snow Day Calculator - Check Your Snow Day Chances | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free AccuWeather Snow Day Calculator to predict the chance of a school snow day. Analyze snowfall, ice conditions, and regional factors to estimate closure probability.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Weather data drives every snow day decision, and our AccuWeather Snow Day Calculator leverages AccuWeather-powered snow day predictions to deliver reliable predictions. Professional weather services like AccuWeather, the National Weather Service, and local meteorologists provide the snowfall forecasts, winter storm warnings, and ice advisories that school administrators use when making closure decisions.

The connection between weather forecasts and school closures is direct: when the National Weather Service issues a Winter Storm Warning (6+ inches expected) or an Ice Storm Warning, schools in affected areas cancel classes roughly 80-90%% of the time. Winter Weather Advisories (2-4 inches) trigger closures about 40-60%% of the time, depending on the district\'s location and infrastructure. Our calculator incorporates these weather-to-closure correlations.

Modern weather prediction has improved dramatically thanks to satellite imagery, Doppler radar networks, and advanced atmospheric modeling. However, winter storms remain among the hardest weather events to forecast precisely because small temperature differences at the surface determine whether precipitation falls as rain, sleet, freezing rain, or snow. A storm predicted to drop 6 inches of snow could instead produce 2 inches of ice, which is actually more dangerous for transportation.

Our snow day calculator helps you interpret weather forecasts into actionable school closure predictions. Enter the forecasted snow amount, select conditions like ice and extreme wind, choose your region type, and get an instant probability estimate that reflects real-world closure patterns.',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do weather forecasts affect snow day chances?',
          'a' => 'Weather forecasts are the primary input for closure decisions. Winter Storm Warnings trigger closures 80-90%% of the time, while Winter Weather Advisories trigger closures 40-60%%.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does ice affect snow day probability more than snow?',
          'a' => 'Yes, even a small amount of freezing rain (0.1 inches of ice) can be more dangerous than several inches of snow because ice makes roads nearly impassable.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How does wind affect snow day predictions?',
          'a' => 'Strong winds cause drifting snow that blocks roads after plowing, reduce visibility for bus drivers, and create dangerous wind chill temperatures for students at bus stops.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'When do weather forecasts become reliable enough?',
          'a' => 'Weather forecasts are most reliable 12-24 hours before a storm. Models improve significantly as the storm approaches with updated radar and satellite data.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between a Winter Storm Watch and Warning?',
          'a' => 'A Watch means conditions are possible within 48 hours. A Warning means significant winter weather is expected within 24 hours and is much more likely to result in school closure.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Check your local weather forecast for expected snowfall amounts.',
        1 => 'Enter the forecasted snow accumulation in inches into the calculator.',
        2 => 'Select your region type to account for local plowing infrastructure.',
        3 => 'Enable Icy Conditions if freezing rain or sleet is in the forecast.',
        4 => 'Enable Extreme Wind if the forecast includes high winds or blizzard conditions.',
        5 => 'Review the probability result and check your school official channels later.',
      ),
      'related_slugs' => 
      array (
        0 => 'snow-day-calculator',
        1 => 'snow-day-chance-calculator',
        2 => 'snow-day-predictor-calculator',
