      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'College campuses have different closure criteria than K-12 schools, and our Snow Day Calculator College helps with college campus snow day predictions. Universities and colleges are notoriously reluctant to cancel classes because students live on or near campus and do not rely on school bus transportation. However, extreme weather events do trigger campus closures, especially when commuter safety is at stake.

College closure thresholds are typically much higher than K-12 schools. While a local school district might close for 4 inches of snow, a university in the same area often stays open unless accumulation reaches 8-10 inches, roads become impassable, or dangerous wind chill temperatures threaten student safety. Commuter campuses tend to close more readily than residential universities because students must drive to class.

When universities do close, the announcement often comes later than K-12 decisions because administrators monitor road conditions closer to class start times. Many colleges have adopted hybrid approaches: cancelling morning classes while keeping afternoon sessions, moving to online instruction during storms, or closing specific campuses while keeping others open.

Our calculator helps college students estimate campus closure probability. For the most accurate university predictions, adjust the region type to reflect your campus setting (urban campuses close less frequently) and consider that college closures typically require more severe conditions than K-12 closures.',
