      ),
      'article' => 'Every winter storm brings anticipation, and our Snow Day Chance Calculator helps with calculating snow day chances by analyzing key weather and regional factors. The probability of a snow day depends on a complex interplay between weather severity, local infrastructure, school district policies, and timing. Our calculator distills these factors into a single percentage that represents your estimated chance of school cancellation.

Statistically, the biggest single factor in snow day probability is snowfall amount. In most suburban districts: 1-2 inches yields roughly 10-20%% closure chance, 3-4 inches yields 30-50%%, 5-6 inches yields 60-75%%, and 7+ inches yields 80-95%%. However, these probabilities shift dramatically based on region. A southern city like Atlanta may close for 1 inch while a northern city like Minneapolis stays open through 6 inches.

Icy conditions are the wild card in snow day calculations. Even a light glaze of freezing rain (0.1 inches of ice accumulation) can make roads impassable and trigger closures when only an inch of snow would not. Our calculator includes an ice conditions checkbox that adds significant probability because ice-related closures happen frequently even without major snowfall.

Wind is another critical factor because it creates drifting snow that can block roads even after plowing, reduces visibility for bus drivers, and drops wind chill temperatures below dangerous thresholds. When wind gusts exceed 35 mph during snowfall, closure probability increases substantially. Our tool factors in extreme wind conditions to refine your snow day chance estimate.',
