      ),
      'article' => 'Regional climate and infrastructure create distinct snow day patterns, and our Snow Day Calculator Canada accounts for Canadian snow day predictions through tailored predictions. The United States and Canada span an enormous range of winter climates: from the lake-effect snow belt of the Great Lakes to the mild winters of the Southeast, from the brutal cold of the northern Plains to the wet Cascade snow of the Pacific Northwest.

Regional differences in snow day thresholds are dramatic. The Northeast averages 5-6 snow days per school year and tends to close at 4-6 inches of accumulation. The Midwest averages 3-4 snow days despite heavier snowfall because robust infrastructure keeps roads clear. The Southeast may close for any measurable snowfall because limited equipment makes even an inch hazardous. Mountain West states like Colorado deal with frequent snow but have high-altitude conditions requiring different closure criteria.

