      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Students and parents frequently ask about reliability, and our Does Snow Day Calculator Work addresses whether snow day calculators actually work transparently. No snow day calculator can claim 100%% accuracy because school closure decisions involve human judgment by superintendents and administrators who weigh multiple factors beyond just weather data.

Snow day calculators are most accurate when the forecast is clear-cut: major storms (8+ inches) in suburban areas produce correct predictions roughly 85-90%% of the time. Accuracy drops for borderline situations like 2-3 inches of snow in well-equipped urban districts where the decision could go either way. The least predictable scenarios involve mixed precipitation (rain/sleet/snow) where road conditions depend heavily on precise temperatures.

Several factors affect prediction accuracy: forecast reliability (weather models improve as the storm approaches), local infrastructure (some districts have extensive plowing fleets while others rely on state roads), historical patterns (some superintendents close proactively while others wait), and timing (overnight storms that end by 4 AM are often plowed before buses run). Our calculator accounts for the most impactful factors: snow amount, region type, ice conditions, and wind.

