      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'The day of the week influences school closure decisions, and our Snow Day Calculator Monday helps predict Monday snow day closure chances by factoring in weekly patterns. Analysis of historical closure data reveals interesting patterns: Fridays have slightly higher closure rates than Monday through Thursday because administrators consider the approaching weekend when making borderline calls.

Monday closures are particularly impactful because they often follow weekend storms where plowing may have been less aggressive. Tuesday through Thursday closures tend to have the most consistent thresholds because they occur mid-week without the Monday catchup or Friday weekend effect. Wednesday closures sometimes trigger multi-day closures if a storm system is expected to continue.

The day of the week also affects how quickly districts announce closures. Monday decisions are often made Sunday evening if a major storm is forecast. Mid-week closures are typically announced the morning of, between 5:00 and 6:00 AM. Friday closures may be announced earlier if the storm is clearly going to be significant, as administrators plan for a smooth four-day stretch.

Our calculator includes a day-of-week input that adjusts probability based on these observed patterns. Friday selections add a modest probability boost reflecting the documented higher closure rate, while Monday through Thursday use standard thresholds. Enter your conditions and day to get a probability estimate tailored to the actual day you are wondering about.',
