      ),
      'article' => 'City-specific snow day predictions require local knowledge, and our Snow Day Calculator Chicago helps with Chicago snow day predictions by accounting for urban infrastructure factors. Major metropolitan areas have extensive snow removal operations including hundreds of snowplows, salt trucks, and brine applicators that pre-treat roads before storms. This infrastructure means urban schools typically have higher closure thresholds than surrounding suburban and rural areas.

Cities that experience frequent heavy snow like Boston, Chicago, Minneapolis, and Cleveland have well-established winter protocols that keep roads clear through moderate snowfall. Schools in these cities may stay open through 4-5 inches while neighboring suburban districts close at 3 inches. Conversely, cities that rarely see significant snowfall may close schools for amounts that would barely slow down a northern metro area.

Urban snow day predictions also factor in public transportation availability. Cities with subway, light rail, and bus systems may keep schools open because students and staff have alternatives to driving. Cities dependent on personal vehicles are more susceptible to snow day closures because road conditions directly determine whether people can reach school safely.

For city-specific predictions, select Urban as your region type in our calculator. This adjusts the algorithm to reflect the faster plowing response and higher closure thresholds typical of metropolitan areas. Adjust for ice conditions and wind as these can override urban infrastructure advantages.',
