          'q' => 'How does a snow day calculator work?',
          'a' => 'Snow day calculators analyze weather factors like snowfall amount, ice conditions, wind speed, and regional infrastructure to estimate the probability of school closure as a percentage.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How accurate are snow day calculators?',
          'a' => 'Snow day calculators are most accurate for clear-cut storms (85-90%% accuracy for major events). Accuracy is lower for borderline situations where the decision could go either way.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What factors affect snow day predictions?',
          'a' => 'Key factors include snowfall amount, ice conditions, wind speed, region type (rural/suburban/urban), timing of the storm, and the school district historical closure patterns.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can snow day calculators predict school closures?',
          'a' => 'They estimate probability based on weather conditions, but the final decision rests with school administrators who may consider additional factors not captured by calculators.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'When should I check the snow day calculator?',
          'a' => 'Check the evening before for the most accurate prediction, as weather forecasts are most reliable 12-24 hours before a storm. Update as forecasts change overnight.',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do snow day calculators work for all locations?',
          'a' => 'Yes, by adjusting the region type (rural, suburban, urban) you can adapt predictions to any location. Northern regions with robust plowing have higher closure thresholds.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
