      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Timing matters enormously for snow day predictions, and our Snow Day Calculator Today helps with today snow day closure chances by considering when weather events occur. School administrators typically make closure decisions between 4:00 AM and 5:30 AM to allow enough time for announcements before buses start their routes. The timing of a storm relative to this decision window significantly affects closure probability.

Overnight storms that end before 4 AM give plowing crews time to clear roads, reducing closure probability. Storms expected to hit during morning commute hours (6-8 AM) dramatically increase the chance of closure because sending buses into an active storm is dangerous. Afternoon and evening storms rarely cause next-day closures unless heavy accumulation is expected overnight.

Multi-day forecasts become less reliable as the prediction window extends. Tomorrow\'s snow day prediction can be quite accurate because weather models perform well 12-24 hours out. Three-day predictions are moderately reliable. Week-long forecasts should be treated as general guidance rather than precise predictions because winter storm tracks can shift significantly over several days.

For the most accurate results with our calculator, check your snow day prediction the evening before when forecasts are most refined. Update your inputs as weather models update throughout the evening. Remember that the final decision rests with your school district and the official announcement is always the definitive source.',
