    'snow-day-calculator-for-schools' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'snow-day-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Snow Day Calculator for Schools',
      'title' => 'Snow Day Calculator for Schools - Check Your Snow Day Chances | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Use our free Snow Day Calculator for Schools to predict the chance of a school snow day. Analyze snowfall, ice conditions, and regional factors to estimate closure probability.',
      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'School administrators make closure decisions that affect millions of students, and our Snow Day Calculator for Schools helps predict school district snow day tool by modeling the factors superintendents consider. The school closure decision process typically begins the evening before when superintendents review weather forecasts with their transportation directors and maintenance teams.

Key factors in school closure decisions include: road conditions along bus routes (especially secondary and rural roads), parking lot and sidewalk conditions at school buildings, forecasted storm timing relative to arrival and dismissal, wind chill warnings that could endanger students waiting at bus stops, and the status of neighboring school districts whose decisions often influence each other.

Most school districts follow a tiered response system. Level 1: Normal operations, all schools open. Level 2: Delayed opening (1 or 2 hours) to allow additional plowing time. Level 3: Full closure with no in-person instruction. Level 4: Closure with virtual/remote instruction. Our calculator helps predict whether conditions will reach the closure threshold for your area.

Enter the forecasted snowfall, your region type, and any additional weather factors. The calculator produces a probability that reflects typical school closure patterns. Remember that individual school districts have their own thresholds and decision-making processes, so use our results as a guide alongside your district\'s official communications.',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do schools decide to close for snow?',
          'a' => 'Superintendents review weather forecasts, consult with transportation directors, assess road conditions, and monitor neighboring district decisions before making the call.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What time is the closure decision made?',
          'a' => 'Most decisions are made between 4:00 and 5:30 AM. Transportation directors may drive bus routes at 4 AM to assess conditions firsthand.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do all schools in a district close together?',
          'a' => 'Typically yes, entire districts close together. Some large districts with varied geography may close specific zones while keeping others open.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a delayed opening?',
          'a' => 'A 1-2 hour delay gives plowing crews extra time to clear roads. School starts late but otherwise operates normally. Delays often occur for 2-4 inches of snow.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do snow days affect the school calendar?',
          'a' => 'Yes, used snow days are typically added to the end of the school year. Some districts build 3-5 snow days into their calendar. Virtual learning days may not count as missed days.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Get the weather forecast for your school district area.',
        1 => 'Enter the expected snowfall in inches into the calculator.',
        2 => 'Select the region type that matches your school district.',
        3 => 'Check ice and wind if those conditions are expected in your area.',
        4 => 'View the probability to gauge whether your school may close.',
        5 => 'Always verify with your school official closure notifications.',
