      'default_options' => 
      array (
      ),
      'article' => 'Regional climate and infrastructure create distinct snow day patterns, and our Snow Day Calculator Northeast accounts for Northeast US snow day predictions through tailored predictions. The United States and Canada span an enormous range of winter climates: from the lake-effect snow belt of the Great Lakes to the mild winters of the Southeast, from the brutal cold of the northern Plains to the wet Cascade snow of the Pacific Northwest.

Regional differences in snow day thresholds are dramatic. The Northeast averages 5-6 snow days per school year and tends to close at 4-6 inches of accumulation. The Midwest averages 3-4 snow days despite heavier snowfall because robust infrastructure keeps roads clear. The Southeast may close for any measurable snowfall because limited equipment makes even an inch hazardous. Mountain West states like Colorado deal with frequent snow but have high-altitude conditions requiring different closure criteria.

Canadian provinces have their own patterns. Ontario and Quebec experience frequent closures during major lake-effect and nor\'easter events. Western provinces like Alberta deal with extreme cold closures more often than snow closures. The maritime provinces face nor\'easters similar to New England but with colder temperatures.

Our calculator adapts to your region through the region type selector. Rural captures areas with limited plowing and long bus routes. Suburban handles typical districts with standard winter operations. Urban reflects metropolitan areas with aggressive snow removal. For the most accurate predictions, combine the region setting with local weather conditions.',
