      'cone-flat-pattern-generator' => 
      array (
        'tool_slug' => 'cone-flat-pattern-generator',
        'h1' => 'Cone Flat Pattern (Template) Generator',
        'title' => 'Cone Flat Pattern Generator - Calculate Slant & Layout | ToolsHub',
        'meta_description' => 'Generate the exact dimensions for a flat cone template. Find slant height, arc angle, and pattern radius for sheet metal or paper cone fabrication.',
        'article' => 'Designing a cone from a flat piece of material is a common challenge in engineering, metal fabrication, and even DIY hobby crafts. Whether you are building a funnel, a hopper, a decorative conical hat, or a precision industrial component, the geometry of a cone requires specific calculations to translate a three-dimensional shape into a two-dimensional layout. Our Cone Flat Pattern Generator is specifically designed to handle these calculations for you, providing the precise measurements needed to cut your material with confidence.

The math behind a flat cone pattern revolves around a few key properties: the radius of the base (r), the vertical height (h), and the slant height (L). The slant height is the distance from the apex of the cone down its side to the edge of the base. This is calculated using the Pythagorean theorem: $L = \\sqrt{r^2 + h^2}$. Once the slant height is known, it becomes the radius of the flat pattern "fan" or sector. However, a full circle of radius L would form a cone with a different base radius. To get the exact base radius you desire, you must calculate the sector angle ($\\theta$).

The sector angle is proportional to the ratio of the base circumference to the pattern circumference. The formula is $\\theta = (r / L) \\times 360^\\circ$. If you cut a circle with radius L and remove a slice so that the remaining angle is $\\theta$, the resulting shape can be curved to form a cone with your specified height and radius. Our tool provides these results instantly, including the arc length and the area of material required.

For truncated cones (frustums), where the top is cut off, the math involves calculating two separate radii for the template. While this specific tool focuses on full cones, understanding these basics is the first step toward advanced sheet metal layout. Using our generator saves time and prevents material waste, ensuring that your parts fit perfectly every time without the need for trial and error.',
        'faq' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'What is the slant height of a cone?',
            'a' => 'The slant height is the distance from the top tip (apex) to any point on the base circle. It is the hypotenuse of the triangle formed by the radius and vertical height.',
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'How do I calculate the template angle?',
            'a' => 'The angle (in degrees) is calculated as (Radius / Slant Height) x 360. This tells you how much of a circle you need for the flat pattern.',
          ),
          2 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'Can I use this for a paper cone?',
            'a' => 'Yes! This tool works for any material, including paper, cardboard, or sheet metal. It provides the geometric layout regardless of thickness.',
          ),
          3 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'Does this handle truncated cones?',
            'a' => 'This version calculates the template for a full cone (one reaching a point at the top). For a truncated cone, you can calculate the full cone first and subtract the top portion.',
          ),
          4 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'What units should I use?',
            'a' => 'The tool is unit-agnostic. As long as you use the same unit (inches, cm, mm) for both radius and height, the output will be in those same units.',
          ),
        ),
        'instructions' => 
        array (
          0 => 'Measure the desired radius of your cone\'s base.',
          1 => 'Decide on the vertical height of the cone.',
          2 => 'Enter both values into the input fields above.',
          3 => 'Review the Slant Height and Sector Angle provided in the results.',
          4 => 'Use a compass to draw a circle with the "Pattern Radius" (Slant Height).',
          5 => 'Measure out the "Sector Angle" using a protractor and cut the sector.',
        ),
        'related_slugs' => 
        array (
          0 => 'circle-calculator',
          1 => 'triangle-solver',
          2 => 'arc-length-calculator',
        ),
      ),
      'distance-between-two-points-calculator' => 
      array (
        'tool_slug' => 'distance-between-two-points-calculator',
        'h1' => 'Distance Between Two Points Calculator',
        'title' => 'Distance Calculator - 2D Point to Point Formula | ToolsHub',
        'meta_description' => 'Calculate the straight-line distance between any two coordinates (x1, y1) and (x2, y2). Uses the Euclidean distance formula for perfect accuracy.',
        'article' => 'In the world of mathematics and physics, finding the straight-line distance between two points is a fundamental task. Whether you are calculating the distance between cities on a map, determining the length of a vector, or solving a geometry homework problem, the Distance Between Two Points Calculator is an essential tool. This calculator uses the Cartesian coordinate system, where points are defined by their horizontal (x) and vertical (y) positions.

The mathematical foundation for this calculation is the Euclidean distance formula, which is derived directly from the Pythagorean theorem. If you have two points, Point A $(x_1, y_1)$ and Point B $(x2, y2)$, the distance (d) between them is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle. The horizontal distance between the points is $(x_2 - x_1)$, and the vertical distance is $(y_2 - y_1)$. By squaring these differences, adding them together, and taking the square root of the sum, we find the direct distance: $d = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}$.

This formula works in all four quadrants of the coordinate plane, meaning it handles negative numbers and zero flawlessly. Applications for this tool are vast. For developers, it can help determine the distance between pixels or objects in a game. For navigators, it provides a "crow flies" distance calculation between two landmarks. Students use it to verify solutions in coordinate geometry and algebra.

Our calculator provides the result in both decimal form and simplified radical form when applicable. It also calculates the slope of the line connecting the two points and determines the midpoint for a complete analysis of the segment. Using this tool eliminates the risk of manual calculation errors and provides results with up to 10 decimal places of precision, ensuring accuracy for even the most sensitive scientific applications.',
        'faq' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'What is the 2D distance formula?',
            'a' => 'The formula is the square root of [(x2 - x1)² + (y2 - y1)²]. It finds the straight-line distance between two points on a flat plane.',
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'Can I use negative numbers?',
            'a' => 'Yes. The formula squares the differences, so negative coordinates are handled correctly and distance is always a positive value.',
          ),
          2 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'Is this the same as displacement?',
            'a' => 'In a straight line, distance is the magnitude of displacement. However, displacement is a vector (length and direction), while distance is a scalar (just length).',
          ),
          3 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'Does this work for 3D points?',
            'a' => 'This specific tool is for 2D planes. For 3D, you would add a third term for the Z-coordinate: (z2 - z1)².',
          ),
          4 => 
          array (
            'q' => 'How accurate are the results?',
            'a' => 'The results are mathematically exact based on your inputs, displayed with high precision to handle very small or very large coordinates.',
          ),
        ),
        'instructions' => 
        array (
          0 => 'Identify the X and Y coordinates for the first point (x1, y1).',
          1 => 'Identify the X and Y coordinates for the second point (x2, y2).',
          2 => 'Enter these four numbers into the corresponding fields.',
          3 => 'The distance will be updated automatically as you type.',
          4 => 'Review the step-by-step breakdown below the result for learning purposes.',
        ),
        'related_slugs' => 
        array (
          0 => 'slope-calculator',
          1 => 'midpoint-calculator',
          2 => 'percent-error-calculator',
        ),
      ),
      'ellipse-circumference-calculator' => 
      array (
        'tool_slug' => 'ellipse-circumference-calculator',
        'h1' => 'Ellipse Circumference Calculator',
        'title' => 'Ellipse Circumference Calculator - Accurate Perimeter Tool | ToolsHub',
        'meta_description' => 'Calculate the perimeter of an ellipse using semi-major and semi-minor axes. Uses Ramanujan\'s precise approximation for high accuracy.',
        'article' => 'Calculating the circumference (perimeter) of an ellipse is surprisingly complex. Unlike a circle, where the circumference is a simple $2\\pi r$, or a rectangle where we just add the sides, the ellipse has no simple, exact formula involving elementary functions. Instead, mathematicians have developed several approximations, ranging from basic to highly advanced, to solve this problem. Our Ellipse Circumference Calculator uses the highly regarded Ramanujan approximation to provide results with exceptional accuracy for various proportions.

An ellipse is defined by two axes: the major axis (the longest diameter) and the minor axis (the shortest diameter). In our calculator, you input the "semi-axes," which are half of these lengths (commonly denoted as $a$ and $b$). If $a$ and $b$ are equal, the ellipse is a perfect circle, and the formula simplifies back to $2\\pi r$. However, as the ellipse becomes more elongated, the calculation becomes more difficult.

The simplest approximation is $2\\pi \\sqrt{(a^2 + b^2) / 2}$, but this can have errors of up to 10%% for very flat ellipses. To ensure professional-grade accuracy, we implement Ramanujan\'s Second Approximation: $P \\approx \\pi [3(a+b) - \\sqrt{(3a+b)(a+3b)}]$. This formula is remarkably accurate for almost all ellipses, with errors only appearing in extremely high eccentricity cases.

Understanding the perimeter of an ellipse is crucial in astronomy (planetary orbits are elliptical), architecture (designing rounded buildings), and mechanical engineering (elliptical gears and cams). It helps in determining the length of material needed for elliptical trim, the fencing required for an oval track, or the path length of a satellite. Our tool provides a fast and reliable way to get these numbers without having to tackle calculus or elliptic integrals of the second kind manually.',
