        1 => 'impermanent-loss-calculator',
        2 => 'satoshi-to-usd-converter',
      ),
      'canonical' => '/crypto-leverage-calculator',
      'article' => '<h2>Understanding Leveraged Cryptocurrency Trading</h2>
            <p>Leverage trading in cryptocurrency markets allows traders to control positions much larger than their actual capital (margin). By using leverage, a trader with $1,000 can open a position worth $10,000 (10x leverage) or even $125,000 (125x leverage). While this amplifies potential profits, it equally amplifies potential losses and introduces the critical concept of liquidation — the point at which your entire margin is wiped out by adverse price movement.</p>

            <h3>How Leverage Multiplies Returns</h3>
            <p>When you open a leveraged position, your margin (collateral) is multiplied by the leverage factor to determine your notional position size. If you go Long with $1,000 at 10x leverage on BTC at $43,000, you control $10,000 worth of Bitcoin (0.2326 BTC). If BTC rises 5% to $45,150, your profit is $500 — a 50% return on your $1,000 margin (10x the underlying move). Conversely, a 5% drop means a 50% loss. This symmetry is what makes leverage both powerful and dangerous.</p>

            <h3>Liquidation Price: The Critical Threshold</h3>
            <p>Every leveraged position has a liquidation price — the level at which the exchange automatically closes your position to prevent your losses from exceeding your margin. For a long position, liquidation price ≈ Entry Price × (1 - 1/Leverage + Maintenance Margin). At 10x leverage, you are liquidated at roughly a 10% adverse move; at 100x, just a 1% move can wipe you out. Understanding this threshold before entering a trade is essential for risk management and position sizing.</p>

            <h3>Fees, Funding Rates, and Hidden Costs</h3>
            <p>Leveraged trades incur trading fees on both entry and exit (typically 0.02%-0.06% for maker/taker on major exchanges). Additionally, perpetual futures contracts charge a funding rate every 8 hours — a small payment exchanged between long and short traders to keep the contract price aligned with the spot market. During bullish markets, longs often pay shorts, which can significantly eat into profits on positions held for days or weeks. Our calculator factors in all these costs for accurate P&L projections.</p>

            <h3>Risk Management Strategies</h3>
            <p>Professional leveraged traders never risk more than 1-2% of their total portfolio on a single trade. They use stop-loss orders to limit downside, position sizing formulas to determine appropriate leverage, and portfolio-level risk assessments to avoid correlated wipeouts. The Kelly Criterion and fixed-fractional methods are popular mathematical frameworks for determining optimal position sizes that maximize long-term growth while minimizing ruin probability.</p>

            <h3>Practical Scenario</h3>
            <p>A trader opens a 20x Long on ETH at $3,000 with $500 margin. Notional position: $10,000 (3.333 ETH). Liquidation price: ~$2,857 (4.76% below entry). If ETH rises to $3,150 (+5%), gross P&L = $500 (100% ROE). After 0.04% trading fees ($4 entry + $4.20 exit), net profit = $491.80. This demonstrates how even modest underlying moves create significant returns — but the liquidation price is dangerously close.</p>',
