      'related_slugs' => 
      array (
        0 => 'crypto-arbitrage-calculator',
        1 => 'crypto-leverage-calculator',
        2 => 'satoshi-to-usd-converter',
      ),
      'canonical' => '/impermanent-loss-calculator',
      'article' => '<h2>Understanding Impermanent Loss in DeFi Liquidity Pools</h2>
            <p>Impermanent loss (IL) is the most important concept for anyone providing liquidity to Automated Market Maker (AMM) protocols like Uniswap, SushiSwap, PancakeSwap, or Curve Finance. It represents the difference in value between holding your tokens in a liquidity pool versus simply holding them in your wallet. When the relative price of tokens in a pool changes from the time you deposited, you experience impermanent loss — and it only becomes "permanent" when you withdraw your liquidity at a different price ratio than when you entered.</p>

            <h3>The Mathematics Behind Impermanent Loss</h3>
            <p>For a standard 50/50 AMM pool, impermanent loss is calculated using the formula: IL = 2√(price_ratio) / (1 + price_ratio) - 1. The price_ratio is the current price of Token A divided by the initial price. If ETH was $3,000 when you entered and is now $4,500, the ratio is 1.5. Plugging into the formula: IL = 2√1.5 / (1 + 1.5) - 1 = 2(1.2247) / 2.5 - 1 = 0.9798 - 1 = -2.02%. This means you have 2.02% less value than if you had simply held both tokens. The key insight is that IL increases with greater price divergence in either direction, regardless of whether the price goes up or down.</p>

            <h3>Why Pool Fees Matter</h3>
            <p>Liquidity providers earn a share of every swap fee generated by the pool, typically 0.3% per trade on most AMMs. During periods of high trading volume, these fees can more than compensate for impermanent loss. Our calculator lets you input the pool\'s Annual Percentage Rate (APR) from fees and models whether those earnings offset the IL over your chosen time period. A pool with 50% APR in fees might easily compensate for 5% IL, making the overall position profitable despite the loss.</p>

            <h3>Weighted Pools and Stable Pools</h3>
            <p>Not all pools use the standard 50/50 weight. Balancer-style weighted pools (e.g., 80/20 ETH/USDC) reduce impermanent loss for the dominant token because less rebalancing occurs. Stable pools (like Curve\'s stablecoin pools) experience minimal IL because the paired tokens maintain near-identical prices. Our calculator supports different pool weight ratios to provide accurate IL estimates for these variations.</p>

            <h3>When Impermanent Loss Becomes Severe</h3>
            <p>IL severity increases non-linearly with price divergence. At a 1.5x price change, IL is only ~2%. At 2x, it jumps to ~5.7%. At 5x, it reaches ~25.5%. At 10x, a devastating ~42.5%. This exponential growth means volatile token pairs in AMM pools can experience catastrophic value destruction during major bull or bear moves. Smart LP providers monitor price ratios constantly and set exit thresholds.</p>

            <h3>Practical LP Strategy Example</h3>
            <p>You provide $10,000 of liquidity to an ETH/USDC pool when ETH is at $3,000. If ETH rises 50% to $4,500, your LP position is worth ~$12,248 vs $12,500 if you just held (IL = ~$252 or 2.02%). However, if the pool earns 25% APR in fees and you stay for 6 months, you earn ~$1,250 in fees. Net position: $13,498 vs $12,500 HODL — a net gain of $998 despite impermanent loss. This illustrates why fee income is the key variable in LP profitability.</p>',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is impermanent loss really "impermanent"?',
          'a' => 'The loss is impermanent because if token prices return to their original ratio, the loss disappears. It only becomes permanent when you withdraw liquidity at a different price ratio than when you deposited.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I profit from providing liquidity despite IL?',
          'a' => 'Yes! If the trading fees earned by the pool exceed the impermanent loss, your overall return is positive. High-volume pools with tight spreads often generate enough fees to more than compensate for IL.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which pools have the least impermanent loss?',
          'a' => 'Stablecoin-to-stablecoin pools (like USDC/USDT) have negligible IL because the prices barely diverge. Concentrated liquidity positions and weighted pools also reduce IL exposure.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How is IL different from regular trading losses?',
          'a' => 'IL is specific to AMM liquidity provision. It occurs because the pool automatically rebalances your token holdings as prices change, effectively selling the appreciating token and buying the depreciating one.',
        ),
      ),
