        1 => 'times-interest-earned-ratio-calculator',
        2 => 'average-collection-period-calculator',
      ),
      'canonical' => '/fixed-charge-coverage-ratio-calculator',
      'article' => '<h2>The Fixed Charge Coverage Ratio: A Comprehensive Credit Analysis Tool</h2>
            <p>The Fixed Charge Coverage Ratio (FCCR) is a critical financial metric used by lenders, credit analysts, and investors to evaluate a company\'s ability to meet all of its fixed financial obligations from its earnings before interest and taxes. Unlike the simpler Interest Coverage Ratio, FCCR includes all fixed charges — lease payments, insurance premiums, and preferred stock dividends — providing a more complete picture of a company\'s actual debt-servicing capacity. A higher FCCR indicates stronger financial health and lower default risk.</p>

            <h3>The FCCR Formula Explained</h3>
            <p>The standard formula is: FCCR = (EBIT + Fixed Charges Before Tax) / (Fixed Charges Before Tax + Interest Expense + Preferred Dividends ÷ (1 - Tax Rate)). The numerator represents the total earnings available to cover obligations, while the denominator captures all mandatory payments. Preferred dividends are grossed up by dividing by (1 - tax rate) because they are paid from after-tax income, making them more costly per dollar than pre-tax obligations like interest.</p>

            <h3>Interpreting FCCR Results</h3>
            <p>An FCCR above 2.0x is generally considered strong, indicating the company earns twice what it needs to cover all fixed charges. Between 1.25x and 2.0x is adequate but leaves limited margin for error. Below 1.25x raises concerns about the company\'s ability to service its obligations during revenue downturns. An FCCR below 1.0x means the company cannot cover its fixed charges from current operations — a red flag that may trigger loan covenant violations or credit rating downgrades.</p>

            <h3>FCCR vs. Interest Coverage Ratio</h3>
            <p>While the Interest Coverage Ratio only measures a company\'s ability to pay interest on debt, the FCCR captures a broader set of obligations. Companies with significant operating leases (common in retail, airlines, and commercial real estate) may show a healthy ICR but a concerning FCCR. With the adoption of IFRS 16 / ASC 842 accounting standards that bring operating leases onto the balance sheet, FCCR has become even more relevant as a true measure of fixed obligation coverage.</p>

            <h3>Industry Benchmarks</h3>
            <p>FCCR expectations vary by industry. Capital-light technology companies typically maintain FCCR above 5x. Manufacturing firms generally target 2-3x. Highly leveraged industries like telecommunications and utilities often operate at 1.5-2.5x. Real estate investment trusts (REITs) may have lower FCCRs due to their capital structure but compensate with stable, predictable rental income streams. Banks frequently require FCCR covenants of 1.2x minimum in loan agreements.</p>

            <h3>Calculation Example</h3>
            <p>A company reports EBIT of $500,000, fixed charges of $100,000, interest expense of $50,000, lease payments of $25,000, and preferred dividends of $10,000 with a 25% tax rate. Numerator: $500,000 + $100,000 = $600,000. Preferred dividends pre-tax: $10,000 / (1 - 0.25) = $13,333. Denominator: $100,000 + $50,000 + $25,000 + $13,333 = $188,333. FCCR = $600,000 / $188,333 = 3.19x — a strong result indicating comfortable coverage.</p>',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a good Fixed Charge Coverage Ratio?',
          'a' => 'Generally, an FCCR above 2.0x is considered strong. Above 1.25x is adequate. Below 1.0x indicates the company cannot cover its fixed charges, which is a serious financial concern.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How is FCCR used by lenders?',
          'a' => 'Lenders use FCCR as a loan covenant — a minimum ratio the borrower must maintain. If FCCR drops below the covenant threshold, the lender may call the loan, restrict further borrowing, or renegotiate terms.',
        ),
        2 => 
