      'canonical' => '/interest-coverage-ratio-calculator',
      'article' => '<h2>Interest Coverage Ratio: Measuring Debt Serviceability</h2>
            <p>The Interest Coverage Ratio (ICR) is one of the most widely used leverage ratios in corporate finance. It measures how many times a company can pay its interest expenses from its operating earnings. Expressed as ICR = EBIT / Interest Expense, this ratio provides a clear signal about whether a company generates sufficient profit to service its debt comfortably. Credit rating agencies, banks, bond investors, and equity analysts all rely on this metric as a primary indicator of financial stability.</p>

            <h3>EBIT vs. EBITDA: Choosing the Right Metric</h3>
            <p>While EBIT (Earnings Before Interest and Taxes) is the standard numerator, many analysts prefer using EBITDA (adding back Depreciation and Amortization) for capital-intensive industries. EBITDA better reflects cash-generating ability because D&A are non-cash charges. However, EBITDA-based ICR can overstate coverage for companies requiring significant capital expenditure to maintain operations. Our calculator supports both methods with a simple toggle, allowing you to compare the results side-by-side.</p>

            <h3>What the Numbers Mean</h3>
            <p>An ICR of 5x or higher indicates very strong coverage — the company earns five times its interest obligations. Between 3x-5x is considered healthy with comfortable margins. 1.5x-3x is adequate but leaves limited room for revenue fluctuations. Below 1.5x signals potential difficulty servicing debt, and below 1.0x means the company cannot cover interest from operating earnings at all — it must rely on financing, asset sales, or reserves to make payments.</p>

            <h3>Industry Applications and Standards</h3>
            <p>The minimum acceptable ICR varies significantly by industry. Utilities and regulated monopolies often operate with lower ICRs (2-3x) because of their predictable revenue streams. Technology and consumer companies typically maintain ICRs above 5x due to less predictable earnings. Private equity-backed companies may operate at very low ICRs (1.5-2x) by design, using maximum leverage to amplify equity returns. S&P and Moody\'s credit rating methodologies weight ICR heavily in their scoring models.</p>

            <h3>Period Annualization</h3>
            <p>Financial statements are published quarterly, semi-annually, or annually. Our calculator supports period annualization — if you input quarterly EBIT and interest figures, it multiplies them appropriately to produce an annualized ICR. This is critical for comparing ratios across companies with different reporting schedules and for detecting seasonal patterns that might mask underlying weakness in annual averages.</p>

            <h3>Worked Example</h3>
            <p>Company XYZ reports quarterly EBIT of $187,500 and quarterly interest expense of $37,500. EBIT ICR = $187,500 / $37,500 = 5.0x. Adding D&A of $20,000, EBITDA ICR = $207,500 / $37,500 = 5.53x. Annualized EBIT: $750,000, annualized interest: $150,000 — confirms the 5.0x ratio. Including $5,000 quarterly short-term debt interest: total interest becomes $42,500, adjusted ICR = 4.41x. The analysis shows strong coverage that comfortably exceeds banking covenants.</p>',
