      'canonical' => '/times-interest-earned-ratio-calculator',
      'article' => '<h2>Times Interest Earned Ratio: Building from Revenue to Debt Coverage</h2>
            <p>The Times Interest Earned (TIE) ratio, also known as the interest coverage ratio, measures a company\'s ability to meet its debt obligations based on its current income. While conceptually similar to the basic ICR, our TIE calculator takes a bottom-up approach — starting from revenue and subtracting operating expenses to derive EBIT, then dividing by interest expense. This approach is valuable when you have access to a full income statement rather than just pre-calculated EBIT figures, providing transparency into the components that drive debt coverage ability.</p>

            <h3>Building the TIE Ratio Step by Step</h3>
            <p>TIE = EBIT / Interest Expense, where EBIT = Revenue - Operating Expenses + Non-Operating Income. Starting from revenue gives you visibility into the operating leverage of the business. A company with $2M revenue and $1.5M in operating expenses generates $500K EBIT. If interest expense is $100K, TIE = 5.0x. But if revenue drops just 10% to $1.8M while costs remain fixed, EBIT drops to $300K and TIE falls to 3.0x — illustrating the operating leverage risk inherent in high fixed-cost businesses.</p>

            <h3>The Tax Dimension</h3>
            <p>While TIE is calculated pre-tax, understanding the after-tax implications is crucial. Interest expense is tax-deductible, so a company in a 25% tax bracket effectively pays only 75 cents per dollar of interest. Our calculator shows both the TIE ratio and the resulting net income after subtracting interest and taxes, giving you a complete picture from top-line revenue to bottom-line profit. This helps investors assess whether the company retains sufficient earnings for reinvestment after servicing its debt.</p>

            <h3>Depreciation Add-Back Option</h3>
            <p>For asset-heavy businesses (manufacturing, transportation, telecom), depreciation can represent a massive non-cash charge that reduces reported EBIT but doesn\'t actually consume cash. Adding depreciation back to EBIT approximates EBITDA, providing a view of cash-based interest coverage rather than accounting-based coverage. Our toggle option lets you switch between both perspectives with a single click.</p>

            <h3>What Makes a Good TIE Ratio?</h3>
            <p>A TIE ratio of 5x or higher is generally considered excellent, indicating robust capacity to service debt with room for earnings volatility. Between 3x-5x is good for most industries. 2x-3x is moderate — the company can cover interest but has limited cushion. Below 2x is weak, and below 1x indicates the company cannot cover interest from operations, which typically leads to credit downgrades, covenant violations, or restructuring.</p>

            <h3>Practical Income Statement Example</h3>
            <p>Consider: Revenue $2,000,000, Operating Expenses $1,500,000, Non-Operating Income $25,000, and Interest Expense $100,000. EBIT = $2M - $1.5M + $25K = $525,000. TIE = $525,000 / $100,000 = 5.25x (Excellent rating). After interest: EBT = $425,000. Tax at 25%: $106,250. Net Income: $318,750. The company earns 5.25x its interest obligations and retains $318K for shareholders — a healthy financial position with capacity for additional debt if needed for growth.</p>',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is TIE ratio the same as interest coverage ratio?',
          'a' => 'Yes, they measure the same thing — EBIT divided by interest expense. TIE ratio is the term used in accounting textbooks, while ICR is more common in banking and credit analysis.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does a TIE ratio below 1.0 mean?',
          'a' => 'A TIE below 1.0x means operating earnings do not cover interest payments. The company must use reserves, sell assets, or obtain additional financing to make interest payments — a serious financial distress signal.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How can a company improve its TIE ratio?',
          'a' => 'Companies improve TIE by increasing revenue, reducing operating costs, refinancing debt at lower interest rates, paying down principal, or restructuring fixed charges to variable ones.',
        ),
      ),
    ),
