      'canonical' => '/average-collection-period-calculator',
      'article' => '<h2>Average Collection Period: Measuring Accounts Receivable Efficiency</h2>
            <p>The Average Collection Period (ACP) is a crucial accounts receivable metric that measures the average number of days it takes a business to collect payment after a credit sale is made. Also known as Days Sales Outstanding (DSO), it directly reflects the efficiency of a company\'s credit and collections processes. A shorter ACP means faster cash conversion, lower working capital requirements, and reduced exposure to bad debts. For CFOs and treasury managers, optimizing ACP is one of the most impactful levers for improving cash flow and reducing borrowing needs.</p>

            <h3>The ACP Formula</h3>
            <p>ACP = Accounts Receivable / (Annual Credit Sales / Days in Period). Alternatively expressed as ACP = (AR × Days) / Credit Sales. For example, if a company has $250,000 in accounts receivable and $3,000,000 in annual credit sales, ACP = ($250,000 / ($3,000,000 / 365)) = $250,000 / $8,219.18 = 30.4 days. This means, on average, it takes customers just over 30 days to pay their invoices.</p>

            <h3>Interpreting the Results</h3>
            <p>ACP should be compared against the company\'s stated payment terms. If terms are Net-30 and ACP is 30 days, collections are perfectly on track. An ACP of 45 days on Net-30 terms indicates customers are consistently paying 15 days late. Beyond 60 days, the risk of bad debts increases significantly — studies show that accounts receivable become dramatically harder to collect after 90 days, with collectability dropping below 70% for invoices older than 6 months.</p>

            <h3>Bad Debt Allowance</h3>
            <p>Not all accounts receivable will be collected. Our calculator includes a bad debt percentage adjustment that removes the estimated uncollectible portion from AR before calculating the adjusted ACP. If you allow 2% for bad debts on $250,000 AR, the adjusted AR becomes $245,000, slightly improving the calculated collection period. This provides a more realistic picture of actual collection efficiency by removing known losses from the calculation.</p>

            <h3>Seasonal Considerations</h3>
            <p>Many businesses experience significant seasonal variation in sales. A retailer with 40% of annual sales occurring in Q4 will show misleading ACP numbers if January AR is compared against full-year sales. Our seasonal adjustment multiplier lets you model peak-season effects. If peak sales are 1.5x normal, the calculator adjusts the denominator accordingly, preventing the ACP from being artificially inflated during slow seasons or deflated during peak periods.</p>

            <h3>ACP Optimization Strategies</h3>
            <p>Companies with high ACP can implement several strategies: offering early payment discounts (e.g., 2/10 Net-30 means 2% discount if paid within 10 days), automating invoice delivery and payment reminders, implementing credit scoring on new customers, requiring deposits on large orders, and using invoice factoring or supply chain financing for immediate cash conversion. Each 1-day reduction in ACP for a $10M revenue company frees up approximately $27,397 in working capital.</p>',
