      'canonical' => '/debtor-days-calculator',
      'article' => '<h2>Debtor Days: Understanding Customer Payment Behavior</h2>
            <p>Debtor Days (also called Receivable Days or Days Sales Outstanding) is a key working capital metric that measures the average number of days your trade debtors take to pay their invoices. It is calculated as (Trade Debtors / Annual Revenue) × Days in Period. For businesses that extend credit to customers — which includes most B2B operations — monitoring debtor days is essential for maintaining healthy cash flow, managing working capital requirements, and identifying delinquent accounts before they become bad debts.</p>

            <h3>The VAT Adjustment Factor</h3>
            <p>One of the most common errors in calculating debtor days is comparing VAT-inclusive receivables against VAT-exclusive revenue. In countries with Value Added Tax (GST/VAT) systems, the accounts receivable balance on the balance sheet includes the VAT charged to customers, but the revenue figure on the income statement excludes VAT. This mismatch inflates debtor days artificially. Our calculator provides a VAT toggle that adjusts the debtors figure to an ex-VAT basis for accurate comparison. At a 20% VAT rate, this adjustment reduces the debtor figure by approximately 16.7%.</p>

            <h3>Credit Sales Ratio</h3>
            <p>Not all revenue generates trade debtors. Cash sales, prepaid orders, and card payments settle immediately and never appear in receivables. If only 70% of a company\'s revenue is on credit terms, using total revenue in the denominator underestimates the true debtor days for credit customers. Our credit sales ratio slider lets you specify what percentage of revenue is actually extended on credit, providing a more accurate measure of credit customer payment behavior.</p>

            <h3>Benchmarking and Industry Standards</h3>
            <p>Debtor days vary significantly by industry. Professional services firms typically see 35-50 days. Construction and government contracting often exceeds 90 days due to progress billing cycles. Retail (mostly cash/card) averages under 10 days. Manufacturing B2B operations typically run 30-60 days. The key is comparing your debtor days against both your payment terms and industry benchmarks. A trend of increasing debtor days over consecutive quarters is a warning sign of deteriorating collection performance.</p>

            <h3>Multi-Period Analysis</h3>
            <p>Our calculator supports different time bases: 365 days (calendar year), 360 days (commercial/banking convention), 90 days (quarterly analysis), and 30 days (monthly analysis). Quarterly and monthly views are particularly useful for seasonal businesses and for detecting trends. If debtor days increase from 35 in Q1 to 42 in Q2 to 51 in Q3, the trend reveals a deteriorating collection pattern that annual averages would mask.</p>

            <h3>Cash Flow Impact Example</h3>
            <p>A company with $2.4M annual revenue and $180,000 in trade debtors has debtor days of (180,000 / 2,400,000) × 365 = 27.4 days. If debtor days deteriorated to 40 days, the receivables balance would increase to $263,014 — requiring an additional $83,014 in working capital funding. At a 6% borrowing rate, this costs $4,981 per year in interest alone. Conversely, improving debtor days from 27.4 to 20 days would free up $48,493 in cash — money that could be deployed for growth or debt reduction.</p>',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between debtor days and creditor days?',
          'a' => 'Debtor days measures how long customers take to pay you. Creditor days (payable days) measures how long you take to pay your suppliers. Together, they determine your net working capital cycle.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why do my debtor days seem inflated?',
          'a' => 'The most common cause is comparing VAT-inclusive receivables against VAT-exclusive revenue. Enable the VAT adjustment toggle for accurate results. Another cause is including non-credit revenue in the denominator.',
