    'acid-test-ratio-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'acid-test-ratio-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Acid Test Ratio Calculator',
      'title' => 'Acid Test Ratio Calculator - Quick Liquidity Analysis | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Calculate your Acid-Test Ratio (Quick Ratio) to instantly evaluate your company\'s most liquid assets against its short-term liabilities. Free business tool.',
      'article' => '<h2>The Ultimate Guide to the Acid-Test Ratio</h2>
<p>The Acid-Test Ratio, frequently known as the Quick Ratio, is the ultimate litmus test for corporate liquidity. Unlike the Current Ratio—which includes all current assets—the Acid-Test Ratio strips out inventory and prepaid expenses. It asks one brutal question: If revenues stopped tomorrow, could this business pay its immediate debts using only its most liquid, easily convertible assets?</p>
<h3>Why Inventory is Excluded</h3>
<p>During economic downturns or sudden market shocks, inventory is notoriously difficult to liquidate quickly. Selling off warehouse stock usually requires steep discounts (fire sales) that drastically reduce the book value of the asset. By excluding inventory, the Acid-Test Ratio provides creditors and analysts with a conservative, rock-solid view of the firm\'s financial survivability.</p>
<h3>Decoding the Formula</h3>
<p>The calculation is strictly defined: <strong>(Cash + Marketable Securities + Accounts Receivable) ÷ Current Liabilities</strong>. Cash is King. Marketable securities represent Treasury bills or highly liquid stocks that can be sold by the end of the trading day. Accounts Receivable represents cash expected shortly from credit sales. Together, these form the numerator—the firm\'s "Quick Assets." The denominator includes all short-term debts due within one year, such as accounts payable, short-term debt, and accrued liabilities.</p>
<h3>Interpreting the Results</h3>
<p>A ratio of 1.0 or higher is the global standard for financial health, indicating the company possesses exactly $1.00 of liquid assets for every $1.00 of short-term debt. A ratio below 1.0 indicates a reliance on inventory sales or external financing to meet obligations, raising a red flag for potential insolvency. However, tech companies with recurring revenue models naturally operate with lower ratios because they collect cash very quickly and carry almost no inventory.</p>',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Acid-Test Ratio the same as the Quick Ratio?',
          'a' => 'Yes, they are identical. Both terms are used interchangeably in accounting and finance to describe the liquidity ratio that excludes inventory.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What if my accounts receivable are uncollectible?',
