    'cash-ratio-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'cash-ratio-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Cash Ratio Calculator',
      'title' => 'Cash Ratio Calculator - Ultimate Liquidity Stress Test | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Test your business survivability with the Cash Ratio Calculator. Evaluate if your Cash and Equivalents cover your short-term liabilities. Free financial tool.',
      'article' => '<h2>Deep Dive into the Cash Ratio</h2>
<p>When financial analysts want to strip away all assumptions regarding inventory liquidity and customer payment probabilities, they rely entirely on the Cash Ratio. This is the most conservative and restrictive of all liquidity measurements. The Cash Ratio determines whether a company could pay off its entire short-term debt balance tomorrow using only the physical cash in its bank accounts and highly liquid cash equivalents.</p>
<h3>The Difference Between Liquidity Ratios</h3>
<p>To understand the Cash Ratio, you must view it within the hierarchy of liquidity:
1. <strong>Current Ratio:</strong> Includes all current assets (Inventory + Receivables + Cash).
2. <strong>Quick Ratio:</strong> Excludes inventory, relying on Receivables and Cash.
3. <strong>Cash Ratio:</strong> Excludes both inventory and receivables. It represents pure, unadulterated purchasing power.</p>
<h3>Formula Application</h3>
<p>The mathematical approach is straightforward: <strong>(Cash + Cash Equivalents) ÷ Current Liabilities</strong>. Cash equivalents refer strictly to assets that can be converted to a known amount of cash instantaneously with insignificant risk of value fluctuation. This includes short-term government treasury bills, commercial paper, and money market funds. Noticeably absent are Accounts Receivable—because during a systemic financial crisis, customers may default on their invoices.</p>
<h3>Strategic Corporate Utility</h3>
<p>Very few companies operate with a Cash Ratio of 1.0 or higher, as hoarding that much cash is highly inefficient for generating returns on equity. A normative ratio sits between 0.2 and 0.5. A company carrying a higher ratio is typically preparing for an acquisition, weathering a severe economic depression, or operating in an exceptionally volatile industry where revenue can halt overnight (such as localized mining operations).</p>',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why don\'t companies maintain a Cash Ratio of 1.0?',
          'a' => 'Cash sitting in a bank account loses value to inflation and generates minimal yield. Companies prefer to deploy that capital into operations, inventory, or stock buybacks to generate higher Returns on Equity (ROE).',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are stocks and equities considered Cash Equivalents?',
          'a' => 'No. While stocks are liquid, they possess significant price volatility. Cash equivalents must have a fixed, known value (like a 30-day Treasury Bill).',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How does cash burn rate interact with the Cash Ratio?',
          'a' => 'If a startup has a high monthly cash burn, their Cash Ratio will deteriorate rapidly month-over-month unless they secure further rounds of funding or reach profitability.',
        ),
      ),
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Input physical Cash stored in checking and savings accounts.',
        1 => 'Input Cash Equivalents (T-Bills, Money Markets).',
        2 => 'Input your total Current Liabilities to see your absolute worst-case survival metric.',
      ),
      'related_slugs' => 
