    'current-ratio-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'current-ratio-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Current Ratio Calculator',
      'title' => 'Current Ratio Calculator - Working Capital Health Check | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Instantly calculate your Current Ratio (Working Capital Ratio). Evaluate your firm\'s ability to pay its short-term obligations using all current assets.',
      'article' => '<h2>Evaluating Solvency via the Current Ratio</h2>
<p>The Current Ratio, widely recognized as the Working Capital Ratio, is the fundamental cornerstone of balance sheet analysis. It is universally applied by commercial banks, suppliers offering trade credit, and angel investors to gauge a firm\'s most basic level of financial health. Specifically, it measures a company\'s ability to settle its obligations that are due within the standard 12-month operating cycle by liquidating all assets expected to convert to cash within that same timeframe.</p>
<h3>Components of the Formula</h3>
<p>The ratio is derived by dividing <strong>Total Current Assets</strong> by <strong>Total Current Liabilities</strong>. Current Assets aggregate everything from cash reserves to raw inventory, work-in-progress goods, accounts receivable, and prepaid expenses. Current Liabilities encapsulate accounts payable, short-term debt, and accrued employee wages. Because it includes inventory, it is a broader and less restrictive metric than the Quick or Cash ratios.</p>
<h3>What is the Ideal Ratio?</h3>
<p>An age-old rule of thumb dictates that a "healthy" Current Ratio is between 1.5 and 2.0. This means the firm possesses roughly double the assets needed to cover short-term debts, providing a comfortable cushion against unexpected operational hiccups. A ratio below 1.0 implies negative working capital—a technical state of short-term insolvency where liabilities exceed assets. A ratio above 3.0, while incredibly safe, suggests organizational inefficiency, implying that management is neglecting opportunities to put excess assets to work.</p>
<h3>The Inventory Trap</h3>
<p>While a ratio of 2.0 sounds stellar, astute analysts dig deeper. If a manufacturing firm holds a Current Ratio of 2.5, but 80% of those Current Assets consist of obsolete inventory that cannot be sold without massive markdowns, the ratio is a superficial illusion. Therefore, the Current Ratio should never be used in isolation; it must be paired with inventory turnover metrics and the Acid-Test Ratio to gain a complete, three-dimensional view of corporate health.</p>',
      'faq' => 
