    'retention-ratio-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'retention-ratio-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Retention Ratio Calculator',
      'title' => 'Retention Ratio Calculator - Reinvestment & Growth Analyzer | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Calculate your corporate Retention Ratio (Plowback Ratio). See exactly what percentage of net income is reinvested into the firm vs distributed as dividends.',
      'article' => '<h2>The Engine of Growth: The Retention Ratio</h2>
<p>The Retention Ratio, frequently termed the "Plowback Ratio," is the fundamental metric that dictates a corporation’s future growth trajectory. When a company generates Net Income at the end of the year, management faces a binary choice: they can either distribute that cash back to shareholders in the form of dividends, or they can "plow it back" into the business to fund research, acquisitions, and expansion. The Retention Ratio calculates the exact percentage of income dedicated to the latter.</p>
<h3>Dual Mathematical Approaches</h3>
<p>There are two primary methods to compute this critical statistic. The raw monetary method is: <strong>((Net Income - Dividends Paid) ÷ Net Income) × 100</strong>. Alternatively, the ratio is simply the mathematical opposite of the Dividend Payout Ratio. If a company pays out 30% of its earnings as dividends, the Retention Ratio is intrinsically fixed at 70%. It represents the lifeblood of compounding internal capital.</p>
<h3>High Retention vs. High Payout</h3>
<p>There is no universally "correct" Retention Ratio; it is entirely dependent on the lifecycle stage of the company. Rapidly expanding tech startups typically boast a Retention Ratio of 100%—they pay zero dividends because every single cent is desperately needed to capture market share. Conversely, mature, low-growth blue-chip companies (like utility conglomerates) often feature low retention ratios (30-40%), choosing instead to lure investors via massive, stable dividend yields.</p>
<h3>Calculating the Sustainable Growth Rate (SGR)</h3>
<p>The ultimate power of the Retention Ratio is unlocked when it is combined with the company\'s Return on Equity (ROE). By multiplying the (Retention Ratio × ROE), analysts derive the Sustainable Growth Rate (SGR). The SGR mathematically dictates exactly how fast a company can grow its revenues using only internally generated funds, without being forced to issue new dilutive stock or assume massive amounts of new debt leverage. Our advanced calculator model dynamically processes these interactions for advanced strategic modeling.</p>',
