    'alimony-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'alimony-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Alimony Calculator',
      'title' => 'Alimony Calculator - Spousal Support Estimator | ToolsHub',
      'meta_description' => 'Calculate estimated spousal support (alimony) using standard legal formulas. Factor in income differences, marriage duration, and jurisdictional rules.',
      'article' => '## Understanding Alimony (Spousal Support)
Alimony, also known as spousal support or maintenance, is a periodic payment made by one spouse to the other after a divorce. Its purpose is to mitigate any unfair economic effects of a divorce by providing a continuing income to a lower-earning or non-earning spouse.

<h2>Alimony Calculation: Navigating the Financial Realities of Spousal Support</h2><p>Alimony, often referred to legally as spousal maintenance or support, is one of the most contentious and complex aspects of divorce proceedings. It is designed to ensure that the lower-earning spouse can maintain a standard of living that is somewhat consistent with what they enjoyed during the marriage, and to compensate for any sacrifices made—such as leaving the workforce to care for children. Because alimony laws vary significantly by state and country, having a professional-grade calculator that applies standard legal formulas like the "40/50 Rule" or specific state guidelines is essential for anyone entering the negotiation or mediation phase of a separation.</p><h3>Common Formulas: The 40/50 Rule and Beyond</h3><p>While many jurisdictions have moved toward standardized formulas to decrease litigation, there is no single "Federal Alimony Law." A common starting point used by many US courts is the 40/50 rule: calculating 40% of the higher earner\'s gross income minus 50% of the lower earner\'s gross income. Another variation is taking 30% of the higher earner\'s income and subtracting 20% of the lower earner\'s. Our calculator allows you to toggle between these common models or use more specific jurisdictional settings, such as the Santa Clara formula used in California. These results provide a robust baseline for discussions with legal counsel.</p><h3>Factors Beyond the Numbers: Duration and Need</h3><p>Alimony is not just about the current income gap; it is heavily influenced by the <strong>Duration of the Marriage</strong>. In many regions, the length of support is tied to the length of the union. For example, a marriage lasting less than ten years might result in support for only half the duration of the marriage (5 years), while a "long term" marriage (usually 20+ years) might result in permanent or indefinite alimony. Additionally, the age, health, and future earning capacity of each party are significant qualitative factors that judges consider when deviating from standard formulaic results.</p><h3>The Impact of the 2019 Tax Reform</h3><p>A critical shift in alimony mathematics occurred with the US Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017. For any divorce finalized after January 1, 2019, alimony payments are <strong>no longer tax-deductible</strong> for the payer, and they are <strong>no longer taxable income</strong> for the receiver. This was a massive change from decades of previous law and has significantly reduced the "net disposable income" available to be split between the parties. Our calculator includes a toggle for these modern tax rules to ensure that your estimates reflect the current legal environment, preventing costly miscalculations in settlement offers.</p><h3>Using the Calculator for Mediation and Planning</h3><p>The best use of an alimony calculator is as a tool for transparency. During mediation, both parties can sit down and look at the numbers together, adjusting variables like "Duration" or "Child Support offsets" to find a middle ground that keeps both households solvent. It helps take the emotion out of the discussion by focusing on the mathematical reality of the two-household transition. While this tool should never replace the advice of a qualified family law attorney, it provides the essential financial literacy needed to hold meaningful, productive conversations about your future financial independence.</p>',
      'instructions' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Enter the annual gross income of the higher-earning spouse.',
        1 => 'Enter the annual gross income of the lower-earning spouse.',
        2 => 'Input the total duration of the marriage in years.',
        3 => 'Select your jurisdiction for specific formula adjustments.',
        4 => 'View the estimated monthly and annual support amounts.',
      ),
      'related_slugs' => 
