    'pizza-value-calculator' => 
    array (
      'tool_slug' => 'pizza-value-calculator',
      'h1' => 'Pizza Value Calculator - Size & Price Comparison',
      'title' => 'Pizza Value & Area Calculator | Best Price per Square Inch',
      'meta_description' => 'Compare pizza sizes using geometry to find the best value. Calculate area, price per square inch, and real crust-to-topping ratios.',
      'article' => '## The Geometry of the Perfect Pizza Deal
When ordering pizza, most people fall into the trap of thinking an 18-inch pizza is only slightly larger than a 12-inch one. However, because the area of a circle increases with the square of the radius (Area = πr²), geometry actually dictates that an 18-inch pizza is more than twice as large as a 12-inch one! Our Pizza Value Calculator is designed to expose these mathematical secrets, helping you determine exactly how much pizza you are getting for every dollar spent.

### Understanding Price per Square Inch
The most accurate way to compare pizza deals is by calculating the "Price per Square Inch." A 10-inch "Small" pizza has an area of roughly 78.5 square inches. A 14-inch "Large" has an area of 153.9 square inches. Even if the Large is 50% more expensive, it usually provides nearly 100% more food. By entering your prices and diameters into our tool, you can immediately see the value breakdown and avoid falling for marketing "deals" that actually cost you more in the long run.

### Factoring in the "Crust Loss" Ratio
Not all square inches are created equal. For many pizza lovers, the crust is simply a handle, not the main attraction. This is why our Advanced Pizza Calculator includes a "Crust Width" slider. By subtracting the crust area from the total area, we calculate the "Edible Topping Area." Multi-topping pizzas are expensive, so ensuring that you are getting the maximum topping-to-crust ratio is essential for high-value orders, especially when dealing with thick-crust or deep-dish styles.

### Real-World Use Cases: BOGO and Delivery
Standard grocery store math often fails when delivery fees and "Buy One Get One" (BOGO) offers are involved. Is it better to get two 10-inch pizzas for $20 or one 16-inch pizza for $18? Our tool handles these logic-heavy scenarios with ease. We factor in delivery fees as a flat cost and adjust the total area based on the number of pizzas included in a deal. Often, the single large pizza is still the winner, but our calculator gives you the hard data to prove it before you click "Order."

### Practical Example: The Party Planner’s Dilemma
Suppose you are feeding a group of 10 people. You can buy five 12-inch pizzas at $12 each ($60 total) or three 18-inch pizzas at $20 each ($60 total).
1. **Total Area of 12-inch pizzas:** 5 × 113.1 = 565.5 sq in.
2. **Total Area of 18-inch pizzas:** 3 × 254.5 = 763.5 sq in.
By choosing the three larger pizzas, you get nearly 200 square inches of extra pizza for the exact same price—the equivalent of almost two additional small pizzas for free!

### Conclusion: Eat Smarter with Data
Mastering pizza economics is about more than just saving a few cents; it is about applying practical geometry to daily life. Using our Pizza Value Calculator ensures that you never fall for the "upsell" unless it actually provides a superior value. Next time you are staring at a menu torn between sizes, remember that circles grow faster than they look. Run the numbers, find the best price per square inch, and enjoy your meal knowing you secured the best possible deal.',
